1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
One instruction still takes k cycles. What the pipeline changes is how often one finishes.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Fill the pipe once, then one instruction leaves every cycle: k plus n minus 1 cycles for n instructions.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
The diagonal is the pipeline: each row is one instruction, each column one cycle, and every stage busy every cycle after the fill.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
The 2023 paper: stages of 10, 20 and 14 nanoseconds, 100 instructions. The beat is 20, and 3 plus 99 is 102 cycles.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Every stage shares one clock, so every stage must finish inside the slowest stage's time.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Between two stages there is a latch, and its delay is added to the beat: period equals the largest delay plus the latch.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
The 2021 Set 1 paper: delays 150, 120, 150, 160, 140 with 5 ns registers. Beat 165, and 104 cycles give 17160.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
The 2025 Set 2 paper: the same recipe in microseconds. Beat 260, 1004 cycles, 261040 nanoseconds, 261.04 microseconds.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Speedup is the old time over the new time, and for an ideal pipeline it is k times n over k plus n minus 1.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Six stages, 25 per cent of instructions stalling two cycles: the average is 1.5 cycles, and 6 over 1.5 is 4.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Two ways to make the beat smaller: split the slowest stage, or accept a slower clock. The 2016 Set 1 paper does the first.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
When the clock gets slower, the stage count is not the whole story: multiply cycles by period before dividing.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
A structural hazard is two instructions wanting the same hardware in the same cycle.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Two standard repairs: duplicate the resource, or stall. Separate instruction and data caches are the duplication.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
A multi-cycle EX unit blocks the stage behind it for one extra cycle per extra cycle it occupies.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
The 2026 Set 1 paper sums those extras over the instruction mix: 15, 10, 10, 10, 5, 30, 15, which is 95.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
A data hazard is a dependence between instructions that the overlap turns into a wrong answer.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Read after write is the true dependence, and it is the only one that exists in a simple in-order five-stage pipeline.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
The 2026 Set 1 paper asks which order causes a data hazard. Read-after-write, and nothing else.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Count the gap in cycles: the consumer must reach its read stage at least one cycle after the producer reaches its write stage.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Forwarding is a wire: take the result from the end of EX or from the end of MEM and hand it straight to the next EX.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
With forwarding, an arithmetic producer to consumer gap needs zero stalls, because EX output and EX input are one cycle apart.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
A load is the exception: its value appears only at the end of MEM, so the very next instruction needs one cycle of waiting.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
The 2021 Set 2 paper prices it: 30 cycles without forwarding against 16 with, so the speedup of forwarding is 1.875.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Write after read is an anti-dependence: the writer must not overwrite before the reader has read.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Write after write is an output dependence: two writes to one name, and the final value must be the later one.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Register renaming gives each write a fresh physical register, which dissolves WAR and WAW but never RAW.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
The 2024 Set 2 paper offers four labels and only one is right: RAW on R3 between I1 and I2.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
A branch decides which instruction to fetch, and the fetch happens before the decision is known.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
The penalty is the number of stages between fetch and resolution: resolve in stage m and you waste m minus 1 cycles.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
A predictor turns the penalty into a probability: an 80 per cent accurate predictor leaves 20 per cent of the penalty.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
The 2022 paper: 1.6 cycles before, 1.12 after, so 1.43. The 2014 Set 3 paper: a longer pipe costs more per branch.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
One formula covers every speedup question: CPI is 1 plus the sum over hazard types of fraction times stalls.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
The 2024 Set 2 paper: 1 plus 0.4 plus 0.6 is 2 cycles against 6, so the speedup is 3.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
The 2020 paper: 1.85 cycles at 2 GHz against 5 cycles at 2.5 GHz. Multiply by the periods and get 2.16.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
The 2026 Set 2 paper: 3.125 nanoseconds against 1.333, so 2.34. And the 2017 Set 1 paper needs the cycle count, not the CPI.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Five steps: find the beat, count the cycles, add the stalls, convert units, then divide.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Three quantities get confused: latency, throughput and speedup. Only one of them is bounded by k.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Two more traps: forwarding does not remove the load-use stall, and renaming does not remove RAW.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
And the fifth trap: a stall costs a cycle, not a stage. Every count in this lesson is in cycles.

